        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How We Chose These Walking Pads</h2>
<p>
            Not tested hands-on; picked from listings, specs and owner reviews. We did not walk on these pads
            or install their apps. We searched Amazon US for walking pads listed with an app, with Kinomap
            and with Zwift, read each candidate’s listing through Amazon’s product API on 24 November 2026,
            and compared it with what this site already publishes about each brand.
          </p>
          <p>
            A pad had to clear four bars. <strong>Its listing had to say what the app does</strong>, not just
            that one exists. Dozens of pads say “with APP” in the title and nothing more, and those were left
            out. <strong>It had to come from a named brand</strong>, which removed many unbranded listings
            with identical photos. <strong>It had to be in stock with a price showing</strong> on the day we
            checked. And <strong>it had to add something the others do not</strong>, whether that is health
            sync, third-party platforms, voice control or automatic speed.
          </p>
          <p>
            We gave most weight to app features that change how you use the pad (tracking that reaches your
            phone’s health app, platforms you may already pay for, automatic speed) and to the pad’s basic
            figures: <a href="/treadmill-belt-size-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>belt size</a>, weight limit and warranty. We gave no weight to star ratings, and little to
            claims such as “AI” that the listing does not explain.
          </p>
        </div>

