          <h3>Control</h3>
          <p>
            The simplest job is starting, stopping and changing speed from your phone, which most of these
            listings mention. It is useful mainly when the remote goes missing. On the UREVO Strol 2E Pro,
            the listing goes further and says the app reads your rhythm and adjusts speed for you, and can
            match your pace to the tempo of a playlist.
          </p>
          <h3>Tracking and health sync</h3>
          <p>
            This is the job most buyers actually want: a record of steps, distance and time that ends up
            with the rest of their activity. MERACH’s listing names both Apple Health and Google Fit, and
            WELLFIT’s names Apple Health. A pad that only logs inside its own app keeps your walking separate
            from your watch’s totals. If your watch is your main record, our <a href="/best-fitness-trackers-for-walking-pad/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">fitness trackers for walking pads</a> guide covers which wrist devices count steps reliably when your hands rest on a desk.
          </p>
          <h3>Routes, races and games</h3>
          <p>
            UREVO’s Smart Walking Pad lists more than 50 global virtual routes and races against virtual
            opponents in 3D, and the Strol 2E Pro lists virtual trails. These are the features that keep some
            people walking, and others never open them.
          </p>
          <h3>Third-party platforms</h3>
          <p>
            Zwift, Kinomap and FitShow are independent platforms that work with many brands of treadmill.
            SereneLife names all three; WELLFIT names Kinomap. If you already use one of them, a pad that
            names it is worth more than any maker app.
          </p>
